  hook with ID `0`.
- Account-level payment-method and card-expiry events now fan out only to the
  subscriptions matching the exact Stripe customer and payment-method/source
  pair. For `setup_intent.succeeded`, the payload's `payment_method` is stored
  (never the `seti_` object ID), and Woo Stripe's setup-intent-to-order link is
  authoritative before any existing-card fallback. This prevents a card already
  used by another subscription from stealing a subscription-specific update.

The Stripe delegate and bridge changes belong to `arraysubspro` and deliberately
consume the canonical, HPOS-safe order relationship helper in `arraysubs` that
was hardened in the ADM-06 fix. Core renewal accounting and its hook payloads
were not changed by EML-02. The core and Pro relationship changes must ship
together. Other gateway callers of the normalizer retain their exact order links
and gain the same fail-closed behavior for ambiguous customer-only lookups.

### Verification

- Historical resolver oracles now map orders `13063` and `13466` to `11959`.
  Conflicting metadata naming `12655` is overridden by the unique canonical
  order owner; a shared-customer event with no order resolves to no subscription.
- A direct call attempting to capture order `13063` into `12655` returned an
  empty result and left all guarded gateway fields byte-for-byte unchanged.
- A unique-customer fixture still resolved successfully, while a synthetic
  multi-subscription order returned no singular owner without explicit metadata
  and accepted an explicit owner only when it belonged to the canonical set.
- Live staging used disposable subscriptions `15627` (Visa `4242`) and `15630`
  (Mastercard `4444`) for the same user and Stripe customer. Paying exact manual
  renewal order `15634` in an isolated customer browser session with saved token
  `17` completed for `$10.00`. Only `15627` gained the order, payment count,
  transaction, and two official-webhook notes. `15630` retained Mastercard
  `4444`, an empty transaction reference, zero payments, its original dates and
  order set, and no new note.
- Mailpit captured exactly the expected customer payment message
  `2o80ogCuFOieNNnHT2ZzYG` for subscription `15627`. Token `31` remained the
  account default and both saved tokens remained present.
- A second browser payment used unlinked ordinary order `15640` for `$2.99`.
  The exact five-subscription before/after state hash remained
  `0399acdaff3b1a8b4797567e858de283a79476ff9caca61166424d3dc72e9509`.
  Gateway logs recorded both successful Stripe events as
  `subscription: 0, order: 15640`, proving an authentic ordinary payment cannot
  mutate an arbitrary subscription through the customer fallback.
- Existing Paddle, Stripe, and BACS orders returned identical canonical and Pro
  normalizer relationship sets. All three changed PHP files passed `php -l`, and
  the scoped diff passed `git diff --check`. Per workspace instructions, PHPCS
  was not run.
- A disposable SetupIntent precedence fixture linked order `15661` only to
  subscription `15662`, while decoy subscription `15665` already held the newly
  selected payment method. The handler updated and emitted the hook only for
  `15662`; `15665` retained an empty update timestamp. This proves the exact
  setup-intent order wins over an existing payment-method owner. All three
  records and the fixture note were deleted after the assertion.
- Final independent review replayed the historical resolver matrix, the exact
  payment-method fan-out, setup-intent order/customer guard, multi-subscription
  fail-closed behavior, and syntax/whitespace checks without finding a remaining
  code or security blocker.

